INT. HOSPITAL VENDING ALCOVE - NIGHT

A humming machine. Bad light. A bench bolted to the wall.

A WOMAN (40s, wrinkled scrubs under a trench coat) stands at the machine, a dollar bill in her hand that the slot keeps spitting back. LUCIA REYNOSO (30s, her younger sister, cardigan buttoned wrong) sits on the bench. ARLO (9, in pajamas, shoes without socks) sits beside Lucia, swinging his legs.

The Woman smooths the bill on her thigh. Tries again. It spits back.

LUCIA
Let me.

WOMAN
It's fine.

Lucia takes the bill from her. The machine accepts it on the first try. The Woman watches the coil turn and a bag of pretzels drop.

LUCIA
It likes me.

WOMAN
It always did.

Lucia hands her the pretzels. She hands them to Arlo. His legs stop swinging.

Her phone buzzes on the bench. Lucia picks it up and holds it out. The Woman takes it, puts it on speaker, sets it on the machine.

NORBERT (V.O.)
(over phone)
Delphine? Delphine, are you there?

DELPHINE
I'm here, Norbert.

NORBERT (V.O.)
(over phone)
They said it was quick. They said she didn't wake up.

Delphine closes her eyes. Lucia puts a hand over her mouth. Arlo opens the pretzels and holds out the bag to nobody.

DELPHINE
Thank you for calling.

She ends the call. Nobody speaks. Then Arlo slides the bag toward her, one pretzel poking out like a small, brave offering.

ARLO
Aunt Delphine? You can have the first one.
